#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "343c42bc-2a8b-5979-9a21-accb89835444" :question/math-academy-id "e-18859" :question/problem "The following equation is true for all values of $x$.\n\n$mx^2 + 5x + 4 = 2x^2 + 5x + 4$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the quadratic terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $m$. On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $2$.\n\nSo, we set\n$m = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212692 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2caeb2c-39cf-595a-8105-67d11bf0e45e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "0538ef73-5254-5d99-b709-b08c9ff89c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-18860" :question/problem "The equation\n\n$ax^2 + bx + 12 = 7x^2 + 2x + 12$\nis true for all values of $x$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $7$. So, we have\n$a = 7$.\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $2$. So, we have\n$b = 2$.\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 7 + 2 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212693 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46822f28-8863-5150-a420-09c61a5c28b1" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Factored Forms"}] :question/id #uuid "802b17c9-3c7d-58b9-b7ee-8632f4f1cd13" :question/math-academy-id "e-18861" :question/problem "The equation\n$x^2 + 9x + 18 = (x + 3)(x + k)$\nis true for all values of $x$, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the factored form on the right-hand side must expand to match the left-hand side.\n\nLet's expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)(x + k) &= x^{2} + kx + 3x + 3k \\\\\n&= x^{2} + (k + 3)x + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the expanded form to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = x^2 + (k + 3)x + 3k\n$$\n\nEquating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 &= 3k \\\\\n6 &= k \\\\\nk &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $k = 6$ produces the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk + 3 &= 6 + 3 \\\\\n&= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side. So, our solution is valid."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9393fc98-0a09-5acf-ad7c-5975ca008d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210469} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb334a2-8889-5128-b085-98908f461dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210470} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf2fb27-8e5c-5563-aa7c-2ec5a1e6fa9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210471} {:answer/id #uuid "519a6167-b997-5944-b6c3-464ebe7bdd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210472} {:answer/id #uuid "79110d02-428d-543e-8afa-1175fc36fb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519a6167-b997-5944-b6c3-464ebe7bdd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210472} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7cd8d0-4ea5-54f6-a2ff-2098e6a6642b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0c83b98-683b-5977-9705-c89b89dc5d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288050" :question/problem "The following equation is true for all values of $x$.\n\n$4x^{2} + bx + 1 = 4x^{2} + 9x + 1$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $b$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the linear terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x$ is $b.$ On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x$ is $9.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nb=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f74c8a-6c5d-58de-9068-4624d614ccd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255491} {:answer/id #uuid "c430f780-eaf0-5c8f-a42d-bf2d2637b0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd08dbe-e60a-5fcb-ada9-c38a3499b7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255493} {:answer/id #uuid "16e383a3-f5b8-5cc9-9cee-8416077b3ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/re